<div style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif; line-height: 1.6;">

    <div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 3</h1>
        <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
    </div>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> In the Library</td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How Do I Find a Book in the Library?</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Engage in role-plays for everyday situations.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe a favorite book or movie and ask peers for their opinion (e.g., 'I read an adventure book about treasure hunting. What do you think about adventure stories?' or 'I watched a comedy movie last weekend. Do you like comedies?'). Can participate in role-plays about common scenarios in the library (e.g., 'I need this book. Can I borrow it?'). Can exchange thoughts on familiar topics, like library activities or films (e.g., 'I liked the book; it was full of surprises.' 'The movie was exciting and fun to watch.').</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows pictures of different types of books (fiction, non-fiction, adventure, mystery) and a picture of a library shelf. The teacher asks students, "What do you see?" and "What is your favorite type of book?" or "What do you like to read?" The teacher encourages simple answers and reviews relevant vocabulary from previous lessons (e.g., <i>book, library, fiction, non-fiction</i>).</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models polite requests related to borrowing books and library rules. The teacher says, "Can I borrow this book?" (with rising intonation) and "You should be quiet in the library." The teacher emphasizes the intonation for yes/no questions and models the short /ɪ/ sound in 'pick' and the long /aɪ/ sound in 'find' by saying "I want to *pick* a new *book*. Can you help me *find* it?"</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I want to take a book home, do I say 'Can I borrow this book?'" (Expected answer: Yes!)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Should we talk loudly or quietly in the library?" (Expected answer: Quietly.)</li>
                    </ul></p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short dialogue between a student (Carlos) and a librarian.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Dialogue:</b><br>
                    <b>Carlos:</b> Good morning, Mrs. Smith. Can I borrow a book? <br>
                    <b>Librarian:</b> Good morning, Carlos! Yes, you can. What kind of book do you need? <br>
                    <b>Carlos:</b> I want an adventure book. I read a good one last week. It was very interesting. <br>
                    <b>Librarian:</b> We have many adventure books. You should go to the fiction section. Look at the *catalog* first. <br>
                    <b>Carlos:</b> Thank you! Can I look for books about pirates? <br>
                    <b>Librarian:</b> Yes, you can! Remember, you should be quiet while you *search*. When you find one, come to the *checkout* desk.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Exchange information about objects, people, and places in simple terms. Ask for and give information about resources and make polite requests.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher asks students:
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Who needs help in the library?" (Carlos)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What kind of book does Carlos want?" (An adventure book.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What should Carlos do first to find a book?" (Look at the catalog.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Where should Carlos go for adventure books?" (To the fiction section.)</li>
                    </ul></p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Speaking: Practicing polite requests with 'Can I...?' and advice with 'You should...', using imperatives, and describing past preferences with past simple. Focusing on target vocabulary (<i>librarian, book, borrow, return, search, find, catalog, fiction, non-fiction, interesting, quiet, helpful</i>) and pronunciation (yes/no question intonation, /ɪ/ vs /aɪ/ sounds).</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ol style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Dialogue Completion:</b> The teacher provides a similar dialogue with blanks for key verbs, nouns, and modals. Students, working individually, fill in the blanks using a word bank (e.g., <i>borrow, quiet, catalog, fiction, read, interesting</i>). Example: "Can I ______ this book? You should be ______." The teacher checks answers as a class, focusing on correct word choice and pronunciation.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Substitution Drill (Pair Work):</b> Students work in pairs. Teacher provides sentence frames and a list of alternative vocabulary.
                            <ul style="list-style-type: circle; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "Can I borrow this ______ book?" (e.g., <i>adventure, mystery, science</i>)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "Yes, you can. You should check the ______." (e.g., <i>catalog, shelf, librarian</i>)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "I read an ______ book last week. It was very ______." (e.g., <i>interesting, exciting, surprising</i>)</li>
                            </ul>
                            Pairs practice saying these sentences, substituting words. The teacher circulates, listening for correct use of modals, past simple, vocabulary, and intonation for questions.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaborative Listing & Peer Check:</b> In small groups, students discuss and make a list of "Rules for the Library" and "Things to Ask a Librarian." They use 'You should...' for rules and 'Can I...?' for questions. Example: "You should be quiet." "Can I return this book?" Groups then exchange lists and check each other's sentences for correct grammar and vocabulary. This activity practices listing information collaboratively and using library-related vocabulary in peer discussions.</li>
                    </ol></p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task in a realistic scenario)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Role-play in pairs.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in pairs. One student is the "Librarian," and the other is the "Student." They receive a scenario card.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Scenario Card Example:</b> "You are a student. You want to find an interesting *non-fiction* book about animals. You also want to *borrow* it. Ask the *librarian* for help. Remember to ask politely. Also, tell the librarian about a *book* you *read* last week and if you *liked* it (use past simple). You are the librarian. Help the student *find* a book and explain how they can *check out* a book. Remind them of one library rule."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students prepare their role-play for 5-7 minutes, practicing their lines and expressions. They are encouraged to use target vocabulary (<i>library, book, catalog, author, librarian, fiction, non-fiction, shelf, check out, borrow, return, search, find, interesting, quiet, helpful</i>), grammar (past simple, can/should, imperatives), and practice intonation for yes/no questions.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pairs then perform their role-plays for the class or for another pair. The teacher observes and takes notes on fluency and communicative effectiveness.</li>
                    </ul></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in speaking, collaborative interaction, and successful completion of the communicative task (asking for and giving information in a library setting, making polite requests, describing past activities).</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment and reflection)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and peer assessment.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the "In the Library" role-play in pairs.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (e.g., <i>librarian, borrow, return, catalog, fiction, interesting</i>).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses 'Can I...?' and 'You should...' appropriately.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses past simple to describe a book they read.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation is intelligible (e.g., correct intonation for questions, clear /ɪ/ and /aɪ/ sounds).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Engages in collaborative interaction with partner.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Completes the task effectively (e.g., asks for/gives information, makes polite requests).</li>
                    </ul></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts on a small slip of paper:
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I said well today was..."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I want to practice more is..."</li>
                    </ul></p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Reflection:</b> In their notebooks, students answer:
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did I use library words like 'borrow' and 'librarian' today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of talking in the library was easy for me? What was difficult?"</li>
                    </ul></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in facilitating speaking practice and interaction? (e.g., clear role-play instructions, scaffolding with sentence frames).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments are needed for future speaking activities? (e.g., more examples for past simple descriptions, additional pronunciation drills for specific sounds).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were students able to effectively use the target grammar (past simple, can/should, imperatives) and vocabulary in their role-plays?</li>
                    </ul></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 4 (Writing), students will use the vocabulary and grammatical structures from today's speaking practice to write a short paragraph or dialogue about finding a book in the library or describing a favorite book they read.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>